        <div class="gmail_quote">
          <div dir="ltr" class="gmail_attr">On Sat, Dec 16, 2023 at
            1:16 PM Muhammad Usama Sardar &lt;<a
              href="mailto:muhammad_usama.sardar@tu-dresden.de"
              moz-do-not-send="true" class="moz-txt-link-freetext">muhammad_usama.sardar@tu-dresden.de</a>&gt;
            wrote:<br>
          </div>
          <blockquote class="gmail_quote"
style="margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex">
            <div>
              <p>Hi all,<br>
              </p>
              In the key schedule (section 7.1) of RFC8446(bis), what is
              the rationale for using <span
              style="white-space:pre-wrap"><i><b>Derive-Secret(., "derived", "")</b></i><span
              style="white-space:normal"> in the derivations of Handshake and Master Secrets? </span></span>Since
              this change was made in draft 19, I expect there should be
              some reasoning of why this was added. <span
              style="white-space:pre-wrap"><span
              style="white-space:normal">Specifically, what are the security implications if this step is missed, i.e.,</span></span>
              <ul>
                <li><span style="white-space:pre-wrap"><span
                  style="white-space:normal">if Early Secret is directly used as the Salt argument for HKDF-Extract of Handshake Secret; </span></span></li>
                <li><span style="white-space:pre-wrap"><span
                  style="white-space:normal">and similarly if </span></span><span
                  style="white-space:pre-wrap"><span
                  style="white-space:normal"></span></span><span
                  style="white-space:pre-wrap"><span
                  style="white-space:normal">Handshake Secret is directly used as the Salt argument for HKDF-Extract of Master Secret.</span></span></li>
              </ul>
              <p>Regards,</p>
              <p>Usama<br>
              </p>
            </div>
